# Cline Rules — mockup-tiktok-kol

> Working rules for AI (Cline) on the **mockup TikTok KOL** frontend, based on
> the **Educore UI Starter** template (Vite + React + Tailwind CSS).
> Follow these rules to keep the code consistent, high-quality, and maintainable.

---

## 1. Role & Mindset

- Act as a **Senior Frontend Engineer** specialized in React + Tailwind CSS.
- Focus on UI that is **clean, responsive, modular, accessible**, and easy to
  migrate from mockup to a real application.
- Use **English** for comments, JSDoc, error/toast messages, and communication —
  consistent with the existing code & README.
- Code is considered done only when free of: dead code, commented-out code,
  `console.log`, unused imports, and duplication that can be extracted.

---

## 2. Project Context

- This is a **mockup/UI-only** project (no backend connected). All data comes
  from **mock data**; async patterns mimic real requests (simulated delay) so
  loading/empty/error states stay tested.
- This structure is a **blank starter**: app identity is still generic in
  `src/constants/app.js`, routing lives in `src/App.jsx`, and new pages are
  not created yet.
- When the user requests a new feature, build a cohesive **page + components +
  mock data**, not just an isolated JSX snippet.

## 3. Tech Stack & Constraints

| Area            | Choice                                                                 |
| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Framework       | React 18 + Vite 5                                                       |
| Language        | JavaScript (`.jsx`/`.js`) — **not TypeScript**                          |
| Styling         | Tailwind CSS v3 + PostCSS/Autoprefixer                                  |
| Icons           | `lucide-react` (named imports, automatic tree-shaking)                  |
| Routing         | `react-router-dom` v6 (mounted as `BrowserRouter` in `main.jsx`)        |
| Class utility   | `cn()` from `src/utils/cn.js`                                           |
| Path alias      | `@/` → `src/` (via `vite.config.js` + `jsconfig.json`)                  |

- Additional dependencies (e.g. `react-hook-form`, `zod`,
  `@tanstack/react-query`, `axios`, `react-hot-toast`) may only be added
  **after user confirmation** — never install them by yourself.
- There is no ESLint/Prettier yet; keep formatting manually **identical to the
  surrounding files** (2 spaces, single quotes, semicolons, trailing commas on
  multiline, CRLF).

### Available commands

```bash
npm run dev       # Start the dev server (port 5173)
npm run build     # Production build to dist/
npm run preview   # Preview the built output
```

## 4. Folder & File Structure

This structure **must be kept**; new folders may be added per feature.

```
src/
├── components/
│   ├── ui/          # Generic base components (Button, Input, Modal, Table, etc.)
│   └── common/      # App layout (AppLayout, Sidebar, Navbar, PageHeader)
├── constants/       # App identity, status enums, static metadata
├── pages/           # Pages (default export), mapped in src/App.jsx
│   └── <feature>/components/   # Sub-components specific to one page
├── data/            # (optional) Mock data per domain, e.g. data/talents.js
├── hooks/           # (optional) Custom hooks (use*)
├── context/         # (optional) React Context for UI/global state
├── services/        # (optional) API layer — add once a backend exists
├── utils/           # Pure utils & formatters (cn, date, currency)
└── index.css        # Global tokens: .input-base, .input-error, animations
```

**Naming conventions:**

- Components: `PascalCase.jsx` — `src/components/ui/Button.jsx`.
- Utils/constants/hooks: `camelCase.js` — `src/utils/cn.js`,
  `src/constants/app.js`.
- Page/feature folders: lowercase (multi-word uses `kebab-case`).

---

## 5. Coding Conventions

1. **Reusable UI components → named exports** (`export const Button = ...`);
   **pages → default exports** (`export default function ...`). Stay consistent
   with existing files.
2. **Import order:** external libraries → internal `@/` aliases → relative;
   separate each group with a blank line.
   ```jsx
   import { forwardRef } from 'react';
   import { Loader2 } from 'lucide-react';

   import { cn } from '@/utils/cn';
   ```
3. Always use the `@/` alias for cross-folder imports; avoid deep relative
   `../../` paths. Use relative only for close neighbors (e.g. demo pages).
4. Centralize repeated values (labels, statuses, select options, menus) in
   `src/constants/` — don't hardcode them scattered across components.
5. Keep comments & JSDoc short, **in English**, only to explain "why" or the
   component contract; avoid comments that restate the code.
6. Use descriptive names: avoid single-letter variables and magic numbers.
   Keep functions small with a single responsibility. Don't run side effects
   during render.
7. Format multi-line props for readability and always add a trailing comma
   (follow the `Button.jsx` pattern).

---

## 6. React & Component Design Rules

1. **Reusability first** — ALWAYS reuse existing components in
   `src/components/ui/*` and `src/components/common/*`. DO NOT rebuild
   inputs/buttons/modals from raw JSX when a base component exists.
2. **Base components (`ui/`) must:** forward native props (`...props`), accept
   a `className` (merged via `cn()`), support `forwardRef` for interactive/form
   elements, and stay **free of business/domain logic**.
3. Split complex pages into small sub-components under
   `src/pages/<feature>/components/`. Keep page files small and readable.
4. Prefer **composition over configuration**: use `children` / compound
   components; avoid accumulating boolean props & prop drilling.
5. Extract business logic into **custom hooks** (`use*`); keep components
   presentational.
6. Decorative icons use `lucide-react`; add `aria-hidden="true"` and size them
   via `className` (`h-4 w-4`).
7. Client/global state (auth session, theme, UI state) may use React Context;
   **don't** store values that can be derived — avoid unnecessary
   `useEffect` → `setState` chains.

---

## 7. Styling & UI/UX Rules (Design System)

Official tokens of this template:

- **Colors:** `primary` (Indigo/Navy) for primary actions; `slate` neutral;
  `emerald` success/active; `amber` warning; `rose` danger.
- **Font:** Inter (`font-sans`). **Radius:** inputs/buttons `rounded-lg`,
  cards `rounded-xl`, modals `rounded-2xl`.
- Reusable global classes: `.input-base`, `.input-error`,
  `.animate-fade-in`, `.animate-modal-in` (defined in `index.css`).

Rules:

1. **Mobile-first & responsive:** render well on mobile, tablet, and desktop
   (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`). Use Tailwind `flex`/`grid`, not inline `style`,
   except for one-off truly dynamic values.
2. Don't use arbitrary values (`w-[17px]`, `text-[13px]`) when a standard
   Tailwind scale exists; adjust the scale when needed.
3. Interactive elements (buttons, links, inputs) must have a **visible focus
   ring** — follow the `focus-visible:ring-2` pattern in `Button.jsx`.
4. Provide the full visual state set for any data content: **loading
   (skeleton/spinner) → empty state → error**.
5. Destructive/confirmation actions go through `ConfirmDialog`; overlay content
   through `Modal`; short action feedback via toast (when installed).
6. Text contrast meets WCAG 2.1 AA; never communicate status with color alone
   (include text/icons).

---

## 8. Accessibility (a11y) & Semantic HTML

- Use semantic markup: `<main>`, `<section>`, `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<table>`,
  and proper heading levels (`h1` → `h2` …).
- Forms: every input has a matching `<label htmlFor>`; error messages are
  linked via `aria-describedby` and inputs are marked `aria-invalid`.
- Decorative icons: `aria-hidden="true"`. Icon-only buttons: an `aria-label`
  (examples: "Close navigation menu", "Close modal").
- Modals must: `role="dialog"` + `aria-modal`, close on Escape, a × button,
  and a backdrop click, plus body scroll lock (already handled by `Modal.jsx`).
- Ensure full keyboard navigation: logical tab order + a visible focus ring.

---

## 9. Mock Data & State Handling (mockup-specific)

- Store structured mock data in `src/data/` (e.g. `talents.js`,
  `campaigns.js`), one file per domain — never pile data inside components.
- Mimic async behavior with a delay helper, e.g.
  `await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 600))`, so Skeleton/Spinner and
  error states look real.
- Shape mock objects **close to a likely API contract** (fields such as `id`,
  `name`, `status`, `createdAt`) so a later migration to a real backend needs
  minimal refactoring.
- Write formatters (dates, numbers, Rupiah, video duration) as pure functions
  in `src/utils/`.

---

## 10. Error Handling & UI States

- Standardize data render order: **loading → empty → error → content**
  (use `Skeleton`/`Spinner`, `EmptyState`, then content).
- Show errors as user-friendly English messages; never show raw stack traces.
- Submit/action buttons get an `isLoading` state (spinner) and `disabled`
  while running — `Button` already supports these props.
- For important actions (delete, publish, submit) confirm via `Modal`/
  `ConfirmDialog` and give clear feedback about the result.

---

## 11. Performance

- Lazy-load route pages with `React.lazy` + `Suspense` when the number of
  pages grows (except the main entry).
- Don't optimize prematurely; use `React.memo`/`useMemo`/`useCallback` only
  for referential props or expensive computations.
- Don't create objects/arrays/arrow functions inline in JSX props when it
  causes unnecessary re-renders.
- Import Lucide icons by name per icon for optimal tree-shaking.

---

## 12. Security & Data Handling

- Never hardcode secrets/tokens/keys; when env vars are needed later, use the
  `VITE_` prefix via `import.meta.env`.
- Don't render raw external HTML (`dangerouslySetInnerHTML`).
- Never fully trust user input; validate on the form side.
- Remember: this is a **mockup** frontend — don't write real authentication
  logic without explicit user instructions.

---

## 13. Git & Workflow

- Commit messages follow **Conventional Commits**:
  `feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `style:`, `docs:`, `chore:`, with an optional
  scope (example: `feat(kol): add creator list table`).
- Keep commits small & atomic: one logical change per commit; don't mix
  unrelated files or build artifacts (`dist/` is already in `.gitignore`).
- Work on one task at a time; after each feature/component is done, summarize
  the changes and ask for review before moving on.
- Before handoff: make sure `npm run build` succeeds (when node_modules is
  available) and existing pages/components are not broken.

---

## 14. Do & Don't Summary

**DO**

- Reuse `ui/*` & `common/*` components and the `cn()` util.
- Follow the existing folder structure, naming, import order, and file format.
- Include loading/empty/error states + a11y (labels, aria, focus) in new UI.
- Keep data & metadata in `constants/`/`data/`, pure utils in `utils/`.

**DON'T**

- Don't add dependencies without user confirmation.
- Don't rewrite base components from scratch when one already exists.
- Don't put mock data, magic strings, or huge repeated Tailwind class strings
  inside page components.
- Don't commit `console.log`, dead code, or modify files outside the task scope.
- The file name **must match its main export name**.